        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ಮೇಕೆ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಮೇಕೆ (mēke) — \"a goat\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಮೇಕೆ (mēke) is shared right across the family — Telugu says it almost exactly the same way — and it names the animal that goes where the ಹಸು (hasu) does not."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A ಹಸು (hasu) is walked to flat ground. A ಮೇಕೆ (mēke) is let up a ಬೆಟ್ಟ, and it will strip the ಎಲೆ off a low ಕೊಂಬೆ standing on its back legs to reach. That difference is the whole reason a household keeps both."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಮೇಕೆ (mēke) is native Dravidian, one word with Telugu mēka (DEDR 5087). DEDR 5087 compares Sanskrit meka."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Two."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
