        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ಕಾಗೆ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಕಾಗೆ (kāge) — \"a crow\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಕಾಗೆ (kāge) is an imitation word: the bird is named for the noise it makes, and the same trick produced the crow-word in each of the sister languages. Once you hear one you have the etymology."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Of all the birds in this chapter it is the one a household actually deals with day to day. Cooked rice set out on a step at the back of the house is put there for the ಕಾಗೆ (kāge), and it arrives within the minute."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಕಾಗೆ (kāge) is native Dravidian, one word with Tamil kākkai and Telugu kāki (DEDR 1425). The entry also lists kā kā, the cawing of crows. DEDR 1425 compares Sanskrit kāka. DEDR 1425 notes that some of the Dravidian forms may be borrowed back from Indo-Aryan."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four."
            }
          ]
        },
